---
import Next from '../components/Next.astro'
import Prompt from '../components/Prompt.astro'
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro'
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
---

<Page
  page="ai"
  title="AI"
  description="Point a coding agent at your OpenAPI spec and it writes typed requests and flows. The prompts, what it wrote, and the check that catches what a test run misses."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">05 · AI</span>
      <h1 class="display">Describe the flow. Then just run it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        <code>specbook init</code> puts a guide in your project and points your agent's instruction file at it, and{' '}
        <code>specbook add</code> reads your spec's security and sets up its credentials. A coding agent then writes
        requests and flows the way your team would: typed from the spec, checked, and runnable on mock data. This
        page is one real run, against Plaid's spec with its 350 operations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" style="padding-top: 8px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="title">Set up the project</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Install Specbook, run <code>specbook init</code>, then <code>specbook add</code> the spec. Adding a spec
          downloads it and reads its security schemes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/install.sh" region="install-plaid" title={false} />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Plaid asks for three headers, so <code>add</code> wrote one profile that sends all three, and a secret
          for each. Plaid accepts OAuth too, so it wrote a profile for that, and one more that fetches a token
          with a client id and secret.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/add-output.txt" title={false} />
      <Snippet
        file="snippets/ai/plaid-config.ts"
        lines="19-31"
        title="specbook.config.ts, as add wrote it"
        mark="8-10"
      />
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/plaid-config.ts" lines="50-55" title={false} mark="5" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The first profile is the default, and the service lists them all. The values come from your environment,
          never from a file, and the mock needs none. <code>add</code> also says what it left for you:{' '}
          <code>Plaid-Version</code> reads like a version and not a credential.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="title">Ask for a request</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>That is the whole setup. This is the whole prompt.</p>
      </div>
      <Prompt text="Add a request that creates a Plaid sandbox public token." />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The agent read the guide and the spec, then wrote one request. It left the config alone, because the
          credentials were already there. It captures the public token, because the next step will need it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/sandbox-public-token-create.req.ts" />

      <h2 class="title">Ask for a flow</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The second prompt says what should end up working. It does not list the calls.</p>
      </div>
      <Prompt
        text="Now make a flow that links a bank account in the Plaid sandbox and reads back its accounts and transactions."
      />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The agent found the four operations in the spec, wrote a request for each so every step also runs on
          its own, and chained them in one flow.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/link-bank-account.seq.ts" mark="18,21,26" />

      <h2 class="title">Values pass by name, and the names are checked</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The first request captures <code>publicToken</code>. The next step sends it as{' '}
          <code>public_token</code>, the exchange captures <code>accessToken</code>, and the last two steps send
          that. The agent wrote each hand-off as <code>ctx.captured.publicToken</code>, not as a{' '}
          <code>{'{{publicToken}}'}</code> string.
        </p>
        <p>
          A string compiles whatever it says. <code>ctx.captured.publicToken</code> has the type the capture
          returned, and the field it lands in is typed from the spec, so a wrong name on either side is an error
          in your editor. A <code>.check</code> step proves the chain: every transaction it reads is on one of the
          accounts it read.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="title">Rename a capture</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Rename <code>publicToken</code> to <code>publicTokenX</code> in the first request, then run the compiler
          and the tests.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/rename-capture.txt" title={false} />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The compiler points at the broken step. The test run still passes on the mock, so a green run does not
          prove the names line up. That is why the guide ends with <code>npx tsc --noEmit</code>, and why the
          agent reads values from <code>ctx</code> and not from strings.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="title">It runs on mock data</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>npx specbook test</code> runs the flow against the mock, which answers from the spec's examples.
          No server and no keys.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/ai/test-run.txt" title={false} />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Setup took a few seconds, and the agent needed about two minutes to get from there to a passing flow,
          with no fixes by hand. That was one run, so yours will vary. Running it against Plaid's live sandbox
          needs a free account's keys, set as <code>PLAID_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>PLAID_SECRET</code> and{' '}
          <code>PLAID_VERSION</code>, and that part is not tested here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="title">Do the same in your project</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Run <code>specbook init</code> and your agent has the guide. It also writes a short Specbook section to
          your <code>AGENTS.md</code>, so agents that read that file open the guide before they touch a request.
          Then <code>specbook add</code> your spec, and its credentials are set up too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/install.sh" region="agents" title={false} />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Agents that read from the web can fetch <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a>, which is the same guide with
          links to these pages. Write your own prompts the way these were written: say what should end up working,
          and name the API in plain words. The agent finds the operations in the spec.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><Next page="ai" /></div>
</Page>
